<BaseLayout
  title="Pest Control Lamesa TX | Dawson County"
  description="Scorpion, termite, rodent and weed work in Lamesa, forty-one miles east of our Seminole base on US 180. Licensed TPCL 0976265, no contracts."
  path="/locations/lamesa/"
  quickAnswer={quickAnswer}
  breadcrumbs={crumbs}
  faqs={faqs}
  service={{
    name: 'Pest Control in Lamesa, Texas',
    description: 'Scorpion, spider, termite, rodent and general pest control for homes, vacant property, farm ground and businesses in Lamesa and Dawson County.',
    areaServed: ['Dawson County'],
  }}
>
  <Crumbs items={crumbs} />

  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">Dawson County · about 41 miles east of Seminole</p>
      <h1>Pest Control in Lamesa, Texas</h1>
      <div class="measure"><AnswerBox text={quickAnswer} /></div>
      <Trust />
      <ServiceMap query="Lamesa, TX" label="Map of Lamesa, Texas" zoom={12} caption="Service area map — call to confirm your address." />
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          US 180 runs directly from Seminole to Lamesa, which makes this one of the simplest runs on the
          route — forty-one miles of straight road with nothing to navigate. Lamesa is a regular
          destination rather than an occasional one.
        </p>
        <p>
          It is also the town on this route with the oldest housing stock, the highest vacancy rate and
          the weed ordinance with the longest reach. Those three facts explain most of the work here, and
          they are worth taking in order.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band tint">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>Five thousand feet past the city limit</h2>
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          Lamesa's property maintenance article contains a provision that does not appear in any other
          ordinance on this route, and people who own ground on the edge of town regularly do not know
          about it.
        </p>
        <p>
          Section 6.02.003 applies to property <strong>"located within the corporate limits of the city or
          in an area lying within 5,000 feet of the corporate limits."</strong> Just under a mile of
          extraterritorial reach. If your assumption has been that city rules stop at the city limit sign,
          in Lamesa that assumption is wrong by nearly a mile in every direction.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>The limit is twelve inches</strong>, and it is scoped rather than blanket: objectionable
          or unsightly vegetation above twelve inches <em>within one hundred feet of any property line or
          within fifty feet of any structure</em>. That scoping matters and is worth reading properly. On
          a large tract, the middle is not the issue. The boundaries and the buildings are.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>You get a minimum of seven days' written notice</strong>, issued by the city secretary
          under section 6.02.004. If the condition is not corrected the city may go onto the property, do
          the work or make the improvements necessary, and charge the expenses to the owner. Unpaid after
          <strong>thirty days</strong>, the city takes a privileged lien on the property.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Above forty-eight inches the rules change.</strong> Section 6.02.007 covers dangerous
          weeds and permits abatement without notice where there is immediate danger.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>And one notice covers a year.</strong> Section 6.02.004(c) provides that a further
          violation of the same kind on or before the first anniversary of the notice may be corrected by
          the city without further notice, at the owner's expense, and assessed against the property.
        </p>
        <p>
          The penalty band is worth knowing too. Violations of the property maintenance article are
          misdemeanours, and the general penalty at section 1.01.009(b) sets a ceiling of
          <strong>two thousand dollars</strong> for provisions governing public health and sanitation,
          rather than the five hundred that applies to ordinary provisions. That is a fine, separate from
          the abatement cost and separate from the lien.
        </p>
        <p>
          The city's Code Enforcement and Inspections office is at 601 South 1st Street. Its own published
          list of common violations is weeds and trash in yards and alleys, abandoned houses and buildings,
          and junk or abandoned vehicles — which is a fair summary of what the work here actually looks
          like.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>The vacancy problem, and why it is a pest problem</h2>
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          Lamesa's population peaked at 12,438 in 1960. The 2020 census counted 8,674, and the most recent
          estimate is lower again. Survey figures put the housing stock at about 4,073 units with a
          <strong>vacancy rate of roughly sixteen percent</strong> — more than double what Brownfield
          shows, and among the highest on this route.
        </p>
        <p>
          Six hundred-odd empty structures in a town this size is not an abstract statistic. It is a
          distributed pest reservoir, and it is the single most important thing to understand about pest
          pressure in Lamesa.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>An empty building supports a population nobody is managing.</strong> Nobody is running
          water, so plumbing traps dry out and open a direct path from the sewer into the structure.
          Nobody opens a door, so nothing gets noticed. Nobody sees droppings, hears anything in the
          ceiling, or spots the gap that opened at the threshold last winter. Rodents that would be
          discovered in week one in an occupied house have a season.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>And populations do not respect a property line.</strong> If the house next to yours has
          been empty for two years, some meaningful share of what you are dealing with originates there.
          You can treat your side of the fence perfectly and still be re-colonised, which is the honest
          explanation for a lot of the "the treatment did not hold" calls in a town with this profile.
        </p>
        <p>
          What you can actually do about it: the derelict-property route is municipal code enforcement,
          which has authority we do not. The city's own violation list names abandoned houses and
          buildings specifically. If a neighbouring structure is the source, a code complaint is a more
          effective instrument than any amount of treatment on your side.
        </p>
        <p>
          What we can do is treat the boundary as the working line rather than the wall of your house, and
          be honest about the pressure. On a property adjacent to a long-vacant structure, an exterior
          perimeter on a schedule is doing continuous work against continuous re-supply. That is a
          recurring service situation rather than a one-time treatment, and pretending otherwise would just
          be selling you a visit.
        </p>
        <p>
          If you own one of the vacant properties: an empty building is enormously easier and cheaper to
          treat than an occupied one. Full access, nothing to work around, no occupancy constraints. It is
          also the property most likely to be generating a code complaint, and the two problems have the
          same answer.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band tint">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>1963 — the oldest housing stock on the route</h2>
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          The median year of construction for housing in Lamesa is <strong>1963</strong>. That is older
          than Seagraves at 1969, older than Brownfield at 1964, and a full twelve years older than Denver
          City. Almost nine in ten units are single-family detached, and mobile and manufactured homes are
          only about two and a half percent of the stock — the lowest share on this route by a wide
          margin.
        </p>
        <p>
          What that means is a town of conventional houses, most of them past sixty years old, built before
          modern sealing practice, and now well into the phase where the original envelope has degraded in
          predictable ways.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Thresholds and sweeps.</strong> Sixty years of this sun and wind flattens a door sweep
          and hardens weatherstripping until it cracks. On a house of this age the gap under an exterior
          door is usually measurable, and it is the most productive single entry point on most properties.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Penetrations from three generations of work.</strong> A 1963 house has had its plumbing
          worked on, its electrical extended, a swamp cooler fitted and later removed, cable and then
          satellite and then internet run through the wall. Every one of those left a hole. Most of them
          were never sealed, and several are now behind something.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Weep holes without screens.</strong> Standard on brick veneer of this era and standard
          for them to be open. They are a designed feature and they should not be blocked, but screening
          them is a well-established piece of exclusion work and it closes a route that runs straight up
          the cavity.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Foundations that have moved.</strong> Six decades on this ground, through drought cycles
          and irrigation, means slabs and perimeter beams that are not where they started. Cracks at the
          junction of the slab and the wall are entry points, and on termite work they are the routes that
          matter.
        </p>
        <p>
          The upside of an older stock is that everything about it is legible. There are no surprises in a
          1963 house — the failure points are the same failure points on every street, and an inspection
          knows where to look before it starts.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>Lower ground, and what it holds</h2>
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          Lamesa sits at about 2,992 feet, near the <strong>eastern edge</strong> of the Llano Estacado
          where the plain begins to break. That is lower than Seminole, lower than Seagraves at 3,353 feet,
          lower than Brownfield at 3,310 and lower than Denver City. It is the lowest of the plains towns
          on this route, and the difference shows up in the water.
        </p>
        <p>
          Dawson County has <strong>no permanent streams</strong>. It drains entirely to playas, with
          Sulphur Springs Draw crossing the county. Rainfall averages a little under eighteen inches, which
          is more than the towns west of here.
        </p>
        <p>
          There is a United States Department of Agriculture soil series named <strong>Lamesa</strong>, and
          its description is the useful part. It occupies the nearly level floors of playa basins sitting
          roughly five to thirty feet below the surrounding plain, formed in sandy wind-blown deposits over
          loamy lake-bed material, and it is characterised by <em>frequent prolonged ponding</em>. The
          series is named for the town rather than typified in Dawson County, so it does not describe the
          ground under any particular Lamesa address — but it describes the kind of basin that
          characterises this country, and the phrase that matters is <em>prolonged</em>.
        </p>
        <p>
          Compare that with the equivalent series named after Seagraves, which ponds <em>briefly in some
          years</em>. Deeper basins, higher rainfall and lower ground together mean surface water that
          stays longer here after a rain than it does forty miles west.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>The practical consequence is mosquitoes.</strong> Not continuous pressure — this is still
          a semi-arid region and the water is episodic — but a longer and heavier burst following a real
          rain event than the towns west of here experience. Pressure typically climbs about a week to ten
          days after the rain and then falls away. Treating on a fixed calendar misses that window
          entirely. Treating in response to the weather does not.
        </p>
        <p>
          The soils themselves are sandy and loam. Sand drains fast, which concentrates the pest picture
          around whatever artificial moisture exists on a property — an irrigation head against a
          foundation, a condensate line discharging in one spot all summer, a leaking spigot. In this
          climate those are not maintenance trivia. They are usually the whole explanation.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band tint">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>What we get called about in Lamesa</h2>
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          <strong>Rodents, more than anywhere else on the route.</strong> The combination is unusual:
          heavy cotton and sorghum ground right up to the edge of town, an old housing stock full of
          openings, and hundreds of vacant structures providing harbourage nobody manages. Harvest is a
          pest event here in a way it is not in an oil town, and the first cold nights of autumn do the
          rest.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Scorpions through the summer.</strong> Striped bark scorpions, peaking in July and
          August. AgriLife entomologists note they move indoors when it becomes excessively dry outside,
          so a long dry June predicts a difficult July. On a house of this age with this many entry
          points, exclusion does more than spraying.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Termites, and the dry-climate myth that hides them.</strong> Plenty of people here have
          been told West Texas is too dry for termites. It is not. Subterranean termites work below grade
          where the moisture is, and a sixty-year-old slab with settlement cracks at the perimeter beam
          gives them exactly the route they need. Older housing plus a longer moisture window is a worse
          combination than most people here realise.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Ants, in forms that get mistaken for one another.</strong> Harvester ants outdoors with
          the characteristic bare mound and foraging trails, nuisance ants indoors following a moisture
          route, and occasionally something that needs identifying first, because the wrong product on the
          wrong ant scatters a colony instead of eliminating it.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Spiders, black widow and brown recluse both.</strong> Present, worth taking seriously,
          not worth panicking about. AgriLife notes brown recluse shelter in firewood stacks, stored boxes
          in attics and low-traffic crevices, and that bites are rare and accidental. Old houses with old
          storage are their preferred situation, which describes a lot of Lamesa.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Weeds, lots and abandoned property.</strong> A larger share of the work here than in any
          other town on the route, and it is where the ordinance section above becomes practical rather
          than academic.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>Cotton, sorghum, and what the calendar does</h2>
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          Dawson County has grown cotton since the mid-1910s and had well over a hundred and eighty
          thousand acres of it by 1960, with sorghum co-dominant from the 1940s and irrigation arriving in
          the late forties. Oil has been produced here since 1934. Employment now runs across the school
          district, the state prison unit north of town, a regional non-profit, the hospital, the county,
          an industrial minerals operation and the city itself.
        </p>
        <p>
          One correction worth making because it appears on a lot of regional pages: <strong>peanuts are a
          Terry County crop, not a Dawson County one.</strong> Lamesa is cotton and sorghum country. It
          matters because different crops mean different harvest timing and different displaced
          populations.
        </p>
        <p>
          What the agricultural calendar does to a property on the edge of town is more predictable than
          almost anything else in this trade. Irrigation coming on after a dry stretch moves things. A crop
          maturing changes the cover available. And harvest is the event: a field that supported a
          population all season comes off in a matter of days, and everything that lived in it goes to
          whatever structures are nearest.
        </p>
        <p>
          Properties on the town boundary experience that as a sudden arrival rather than a gradual
          increase, and they experience it at roughly the same point each year. Which means it can be
          planned for. Exclusion work done in late summer, before the fields come off, is worth
          considerably more than trapping done in October after the population is already inside.
        </p>
        <p>
          Commercial and institutional property runs on its own logic. Food service, healthcare and
          anything with a public inspection regime needs documentation as much as it needs treatment — the
          service record is the thing an inspector asks to see. If you are buying service for a business
          here, say so on the first call, because the paperwork side is worth sorting before rather than
          after.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band tint">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>Services in Lamesa</h2>
      <div class="grid c3" style="margin-top:1.2rem">
        {SERVICES.filter((s) => s.priority <= 2).map((s) => (
          <a class="card" href={`/services/${s.slug}/`}>
            <h3>{s.name}</h3><p>{s.summary}</p>
          </a>
        ))}
      </div>
      <div class="measure" style="margin-top:2rem">
        <p>
          <strong>No contracts.</strong> Recurring service runs bi-monthly or quarterly, priced on the size
          of the property, the pressure and the frequency, with a higher initial visit. Termite, bed bug,
          mosquito, lawn, brush and junk work is individually quoted after an inspection.
        </p>
        <p>
          Covered by the <a href="/our-guarantee/">30-day service guarantee</a>, with termite work
          carrying its own one-year re-treatment warranty. Military discount. Cash, check, card in person
          or online, Venmo, CashApp or Zelle. Se habla español.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>There are local companies here, and we are not going to pretend otherwise</h2>
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          On several other pages on this site we point out that no pest control company is physically
          based in the town. In Lamesa that would not be true, and saying it would be the sort of thing
          this whole site exists not to do. There are businesses listed at Lamesa street addresses.
        </p>
        <p>
          So the argument here has to be different, and it has to be about something other than proximity.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Check the licence.</strong> Ours is TPCL 0976265, published on every page of this site,
          and it can be verified against the Texas Department of Agriculture's own records by anybody in
          about two minutes. Do it for us and do it for whoever else you are considering, and check that
          the categories on the licence cover the work being quoted — a termite job needs the termite
          category. That is the only piece of due diligence in this trade that is both free and
          conclusive.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Read the guarantee.</strong> Ours is written out in full on its own page, with a
          duration, a covered scope, a stated remedy and an explicit list of exclusions. Ask for the same
          from anyone else. A guarantee without a number in it is not a guarantee.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Ask who does the work.</strong> Here it is the owner and licence holder personally, on
          every job. That is not automatically better than a company with technicians, but it is a real
          difference and it is worth knowing which you are buying.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Compare the scope, not the number.</strong> Covered pests, whether outbuildings are
          included, exterior only or interior as well, and the recurring price rather than the
          introductory one. Two quotes that look ten percent apart are frequently not describing the same
          job at all.
        </p>
        <p>
          If somebody else is the better fit for your property after all that, take them. A fair
          comparison lost is a better outcome for everybody than a job won because the customer could not
          see what they were buying.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <Faq items={faqs} heading="Lamesa pest control questions" />
  <Cta
    market="Lamesa"
    line="Forty-one miles east on 180. Tell him what you are seeing, whether the property is occupied or has been sitting, and if a city notice has arrived, read him the date on it."
  />
</BaseLayout>
